# Permutations MCP — Agent Rules (v1.0.0)

## Setup — Connect The MCP Server First

The 12 tools only exist once the MCP server is registered with your agent or IDE. Pick your environment:

### Claude Code (CLI)
**Global (recommended — works in every project automatically):** add to `~/.claude/settings.json`:
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "permutations": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/permutations/dist/server.js"],
      "env": { "GROQ_API_KEY": "your-key" }
    }
  }
}
```
**Project-scoped:** create `.mcp.json` in any project root — only active in that directory.

Alternatively run `claude mcp add` from the CLI — Claude Code writes the config for you and prompts whether to save globally or per-project.

### Cursor
**Per-project only** — no global option. Create `.cursor/mcp.json` in each project root (or copy it once into any repo you want to use Permutations from):
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "permutations": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/permutations/dist/server.js"],
      "env": { "GROQ_API_KEY": "your-key" }
    }
  }
}
```

### Claude Desktop
**Always global** — single user config, applies to all conversations automatically.
Edit `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` (Mac) or `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json` (Windows):
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "permutations": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/permutations/dist/server.js"],
      "env": { "GROQ_API_KEY": "your-key" }
    }
  }
}
```

### Windsurf
**Always global** — applies to all Windsurf workspaces automatically.
Edit `~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json`:
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "permutations": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/permutations/dist/server.js"],
      "env": { "GROQ_API_KEY": "your-key" }
    }
  }
}
```

### VS Code (Copilot / Cline / Continue)
Already configured in `.vscode/mcp.json` in this repo — no action needed if you're in the project folder.

---

### Notes
- **Path**: use the absolute path to `dist/server.js`, not a relative path — agents run from different working directories
- **API key**: set **any one** of the following — the server auto-detects whichever is present:
  | Variable | Provider | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `GROQ_API_KEY` | Groq | Free tier, fast inference — good default |
  | `OPENAI_API_KEY` | OpenAI | GPT-4o / GPT-4-turbo |
  | `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | Anthropic | Claude 3.5+ |
  | `GEMINI_API_KEY` | Google Gemini | Gemini 1.5 Pro / Flash |
  | `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` | OpenRouter | Access to 100+ models under one key |
  | `HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY` | HuggingFace | Open-source models |
  You do not need all of them — one is enough. Skip entirely only if using `offline: true`.
- **Build first**: run `npm install && npm run build` in the permutations directory before registering the server — the config points to `dist/server.js`, not `src/`
- **Verify**: after registering, the tools appear as `permutations__generate_design_genome`, `permutations__validate_design`, etc. If they don't appear, the server path or API key is wrong.

---

These rules govern how AI coding agents must interact with this codebase. The Permutations MCP server generates a **four-layer SHA-256 genome chain** — CreatorGenome (L0, 16 chromosomes) → DesignGenome (L1, 32 chromosomes) → EcosystemGenome (L2, 11 chromosomes) → CivilizationGenome (L3, 10 chromosomes) — across a **14-tier biological complexity model** (abiotic → prokaryotic → protist → bryophyte → vascular_flora → invertebrate_fauna → ectotherm_fauna → endotherm_fauna → tribal → city_state → nation_state → empire → network → singularity). **No aesthetic values may be invented manually.**

This tool is a **design constitution generator**, not a code generator. It produces genome constraints, CSS tokens, component relationships, and architecture direction. **You — the agent — write the actual code** from those constraints.

**Key v2.0.0 changes:**
- **L0 Creator DNA**: New generative layer for simulated designer personas — each with unique voice, biography, and creative instincts
- **L0→L1 Bridge**: Creator Personas influence Design Genome generation (chaos → physics, aesthetic → hue, etc.)
- Complexity tier is now vocabulary-invariant — computed from what the product DOES, not from keywords in the description
- EcosystemGenome (L2) and CivilizationGenome (L3) are fully wired — generate_ecosystem and generate_civilization return genome data that feeds the next layer
- Governance model (L3) overrides state topology; knowledge model (L3) overrides routing pattern

---

## Tool Workflow (12 tools, in order)

### Standard Flow (L1 Entry)
```
STEP 1  generate_design_genome         ← always start here
STEP 2  generate_component_tokens      ← derive per-component CSS tokens (L0.8 layer)
STEP 3  generate_design_brief          ← read before writing any code
STEP 4  generate_ecosystem             ← (optional) when building a component library
STEP 5  generate_civilization          ← (optional) when complexity ≥ 0.68
FINAL   validate_design                ← run before shipping any CSS/HTML
```

### Creator Flow (L0 Entry — Simulated Designer Personas)
```
STEP 1  generate_creator_genome        ← create 16-chromosome creator DNA
STEP 2  generate_persona               ← decode into designer persona (LLM)
STEP 3  generate_design_through_persona ← persona interprets intent → L1 genome
STEP 4  generate_component_tokens      ← derive per-component CSS tokens (L0.8 layer)
STEP 5  generate_design_brief          ← read before writing any code
...
```

### Utility Tools
```
ALTERNATIVE  extract_genome_from_url     ← use instead of STEP 1 when you have a reference site
EXPORT       generate_formats            ← export tokens to Figma/Style Dictionary after STEP 1
ITERATE      update_design_genome        ← adjust chromosomes after STEP 1 ("make it warmer")
```

The response from `generate_design_genome` includes `suggested_next` — read it. It tells you exactly which tools to call next based on the genome's complexity.

---

## Rule 1 — Always Call The Engine First

Before creating any new UI component, page, section or design token, you MUST call `generate_design_genome`.

```json
{
  "tool": "generate_design_genome",
  "arguments": {
    "intent": "<describe the component or page in plain language>",
    "seed": "<project-name>-<component-name>-<timestamp>",
    "project_context": "<overarching brand or product narrative>",
    "brand_asset_paths": ["</absolute/path/to/logo.png>", "</absolute/path/to/brand-guidelines.pdf>"]
  }
}
```

`project_context` and `brand_asset_paths` are optional but improve accuracy.

**REQUIREMENT:** One LLM API key MUST be set: GROQ_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY, OPENROUTER_API_KEY, or HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY.

---

## Rule 2 — Read The Genome Report

Every `generate_design_genome` response includes a `genome_report` field — a markdown document explaining:
- Which sector was detected from your intent
- Which chromosomes were sequenced and their values
- What color constraints are in effect (including dark mode variants)
- The suggested workflow for what to call next

**Read this report before writing any code.** The genome is a black box without it.

---

## Rule 3 — Generate Component Tokens Before Writing Any Component Code

After `generate_design_genome` (or `generate_design_through_persona`), call `generate_component_tokens` to get per-component CSS token decisions derived from the genome's continuous latent coordinates.

```json
{
  "tool": "generate_component_tokens",
  "arguments": {
    "genome": "<the L1 DesignGenome returned by generate_design_genome>",
    "component_specs": [
      { "name": "primary-cta", "description": "hero call-to-action button, initiates purchase flow, primary action" },
      { "name": "product-card", "description": "product listing card with image, price, rating — appears in a grid" },
      { "name": "nav-link", "description": "top navigation anchor link, routing between sections" },
      { "name": "filter-chip", "description": "compact filterable tag, toggleable, appears in rows of many" }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Name and describe the actual components your product uses.** The engine infers semantic properties (interactivity, elevation, density, media, circularity) from the description — no fixed component type list. A `vehicle-listing-card` on an ecommerce site and a `story-card` on an editorial site get different tokens because their descriptions contain different signals.

The response includes:
- `component_tokens` — per-component `filled` / `ghost` / `flat` variants with all interaction states (`default`, `hover`, `active`, `focus`, `disabled`)
- `css_variables` — a `:root {}` block of `--genome-*` custom properties to paste into your global stylesheet
- `vec` — the `ComponentDecisionVector` with every continuous genome parameter
- `motif` — the signature typographic device (separator character, hover indicator)

Each component entry includes a `specs` object with the full output from every engine:
- `fill` — gradient type, stops, noise overlay, gradient-text flag
- `blend` — mix-blend-mode, element opacity, background-blend-mode
- `filter` — element filter, image/media filter, backdrop-filter
- `stroke` — border width/style/position, gradient border-image
- `spacing` — padding shorthand, gap, individual side values
- `innerShadow` — inset box-shadow layers
- `textShadow` — display heading text-shadow

Each entry also has a `rationale` field — read it to understand why values were chosen.

**Do not invent component-specific CSS values not present in the token response.**

---

## Rule 4 — Apply The Generated CSS Tokens

The genome response includes a `css` field — the full design token stylesheet. Inject this into your project:

- Copy CSS custom properties under `:root` into your `index.css` or global stylesheet
- Use `var(--color-primary)` for light mode, `var(--color-primary-interactive)` for dark mode buttons
- Use `var(--genome-easing)` and `var(--genome-duration)` for all animations
- Use `var(--color-surface-0)` through `--color-surface-3` for backgrounds

**Do not invent values not present in the genome.**

---

## Rule 5 — Use The Genome's Library Selections

The genome response includes `library_selections` — chromosome-derived library picks for icons, animation, state management, and styling. **You MUST use these specific packages.** Do not substitute your own preferences.

```
genome.library_selections.icon_library         → install this icon package
genome.library_selections.animation_library    → install this animation library
genome.library_selections.state_management.primary  → use this for state
genome.library_selections.styling_system.primary    → use this styling approach
```

Each entry includes `package`, `import_example`, `install`, and `note` fields. Read them.

**`generate_ecosystem` (Step 4) adds more:**
```
ecosystem.libraries.animation    → component-level animation library
ecosystem.libraries.organism     → UI component library (primary + alternative)
ecosystem.libraries.interaction  → gesture/drag/interaction library
ecosystem.libraries.charts       → chart library (only if data-heavy)
```

These selections are deterministic — different genomes pick different libraries by design. An icon library mismatch (e.g., using Lucide when the genome selected Heroicons) is a chromosome violation that `validate_design` will flag.

---

## Rule 6 — Use Component Tokens For Every Property They Cover

When `generate_component_tokens` has been called, use its values — not invented CSS — for:

| Property | Source in token response |
|---|---|
| `border-radius` | `component_tokens[name].borderRadiusPx` |
| `box-shadow` | `component_tokens[name].filled.default.boxShadow` |
| `padding` | `component_tokens[name].padding` |
| `gap` | `component_tokens[name].gap` |
| `transition` | `component_tokens[name].filled.default.transition` |
| `transform` (hover) | `component_tokens[name].filled.hover.transform` |
| `letter-spacing` | `component_tokens[name].filled.default.letterSpacing` |
| `filter` | `component_tokens[name].specs.filter.elementFilter` |
| `mix-blend-mode` | `component_tokens[name].specs.blend.mixBlendMode` |
| `border` | `component_tokens[name].specs.stroke.border` |
| Gradient fill | `component_tokens[name].specs.fill.background` |
| Image treatment | `component_tokens[name].specs.filter.imageFilter` (apply to `img`/`video`) |
| Text shadow | `component_tokens[name].specs.textShadow.css` (apply to headings) |
| Inner shadow | composed into `boxShadow` already |
| Noise overlay | `component_tokens[name].specs.fill.noiseOverlay` — implement as a `::after` pseudo-element |

---

## Rule 7 — Respect Forbidden Patterns

The `genome.constraints.forbiddenPatterns` array contains patterns you are **strictly prohibited from using**.

Common examples:
| Pattern | What to avoid |
|---|---|
| `large_hero_images` | No full-bleed background images in hero sections |
| `scroll_animations` | No scroll-triggered fade/parallax effects |
| `bounce_animations` | No spring-easing on bouncy elements |
| `rounded_cards` | No heavily rounded cards (border-radius must be genome-defined) |
| `heavy_blur_effects` | No backdrop-filter or gaussian blur greater than genome intensity |
| `generous_whitespace` | No padding > genome-unit × 2 between sections |

Run `validate_design` before shipping to catch violations automatically.

---

## Rule 8 — Respect Required Patterns

The `genome.constraints.requiredPatterns` array contains patterns you **must implement**.

Common examples:
| Pattern | What to implement |
|---|---|
| `high_contrast_text` | All body text must meet the `minContrastRatio` from ch17_accessibility |
| `tabular_numerals` | All numbers must use `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` |
| `compact_base_spacing` | Use `genome-unit` (from ch2) as the base spacing unit everywhere |

---

## Rule 9 — Typography Must Use Genome Values

Do not invent font sizes. Use the values from `ch3_type_display` (headings) and `ch4_type_body` (body text):
- `text-display` for hero headings
- `text-h1`, `text-h2`, `text-h3` for hierarchy
- `text-body` for paragraphs
- `text-small` for captions and metadata

Do not use font families not defined in the genome.

### Font Providers

Pass `font_provider` to `generate_design_genome` to control which catalog the engine draws from:

| Provider | Source | When To Use |
|---|---|---|
| `"google"` | Google Fonts (1000+ fonts) | Default. CDN import included. |
| `"bunny"` | Bunny Fonts (mirrors Google) | Privacy-focused. Same catalog as Google. |
| `"fontshare"` | Fontshare (~100 fonts) | High-quality editorial/display fonts (Satoshi, Cabinet Grotesk, etc.) |
| `"none"` | No CDN | You manage font loading. Returns family name only — no `@import` URL. |

**Do not pass a font name that belongs to a different provider.** If `extract_genome_from_url` returns a Fontshare font (e.g. `satoshi`, `cabinet-grotesk`), pass `font_provider: "fontshare"` — not `"google"`.

---

## Rule 10 — Color Is Derived From The Genome

The primary color is defined by `ch5_color_primary.hex`. Never use hardcoded hex values.

Dark mode: use `ch5_color_primary.darkModeHex` (lightness 58–74%) for buttons and interactive elements on dark surfaces. The base `hex` (lightness 22–35%) is invisible on dark backgrounds.

Background colors come from `ch6_color_temp.surfaceStack`. Use `--color-surface-0` through `--color-surface-3`.

---

## Rule 11 — Motion Must Respect The Genome

Use `transition-timing-function: var(--genome-easing)` and `transition-duration: var(--genome-duration)` for all animations.
If `ch17_accessibility.respectMotionPreference` is `true`, wrap all animations with `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)`.

---

## Rule 12 — Ecosystem And Civilization Return Specs, Not Code

`generate_ecosystem` returns a component hierarchy (microbial/flora/fauna) with prop contracts, containment relationships, and color treatments. **You write the components from these specs.** The tool does not generate TSX.

`generate_civilization` returns architecture direction: state topology, routing patterns, token inheritance. **You implement the architecture from these constraints.** The tool does not generate application files by default.

To opt into code generation: pass `generate_code: true` to `generate_civilization`. This is an explicit opt-in — default is `false`.

---

## Rule 13 — Validate Before Shipping

Always call `validate_design` with your final CSS and HTML before shipping. It checks:
- Forbidden pattern violations from the genome constraints
- Dark pattern detection (AI slop tells, generic shadow stacks, gradient text)
- Contrast and accessibility requirements

A `slop_score: 0` and `valid: true` is the target.

---

## Design Exploration Workflow

Permutations has three modes of use — not just generation:

**Direction** — You know what you want to build. Call `generate_design_genome` → `generate_component_tokens` → `generate_design_brief` → implement.

**Constraint** — You're building from an existing genome. Call `validate_design` before shipping. Use `genome.constraints.forbiddenPatterns` and `requiredPatterns` to guard your implementation.

**Exploration** — You're figuring out what you want. Two paths:
- "I love this site, build something inspired by it" → call `extract_genome_from_url` instead of STEP 1
- "Make it warmer / more editorial / change sector to fintech" → call `update_design_genome` with the specific chromosome to mutate — do not regenerate from scratch

---

## When To Re-Run The Engine

- When starting a new feature area (e.g. switching from landing page to dashboard)
- When the user provides brand assets (logos, style guides)
- When the user changes the direction ("make it warmer", "change sector to fintech") — use `update_design_genome`
- Never re-run with the same seed for the same component unless you want deterministic regeneration
